    "Year 12 - Story 2: The Missing Friend"
    "Before the spaceship model contest, Stella and Mina had a routine."
    "It was not an official routine."
    "They had never written it down or made any promises about it."
    "But it happened often enough that Stella had started to think of it as something that simply existed."
    "After school, Mina would wait near the old gate."
    "Stella would pretend she had not been looking for her."
    "Then they would walk through town together."
    "Sometimes they talked about homework, sometimes they complained about teachers."
    "Sometimes Mina talked for ten minutes about a dream she had, and Stella spent most of the walk trying to understand how a duck, a library, and a broken elevator were supposed to be connected."
    "Then, if they had enough time, they would stop at Home Plate."
    "They usually took the same table, the one close enough to the bookshelf that Stella could read the titles from her seat, but far enough from the counter that Mina could whisper things without feeling too obvious."
    "They did not always order a full meal, but sometimes they shared a dessert, or split a plate of fries."
    "Sometimes they only sat there with two drinks and one cookie each, acting like this was a perfectly reasonable way to spend an afternoon."
    "Stella never said she liked the routine, that would have made it sound too important."
    "But she remembered it."
    "Like which chair Mina liked."
    "Or that Mina always broke cookies into smaller pieces before eating them."
    "She remembered that Mina said the window seat was better in winter because the light made her hands feel warm."
    "It’s all small things, unimportant things."
    "Except Stella kept noticing them anyway."
    "But then the contest happened."
    "And after the contest, Mina changed."
    "Not in any big dramatic way."
    "She did not shout at Stella, or said anything cruel."
    "She just simply stopped waiting by the gate."
    "The first time, Stella told herself Mina had probably gone home early."
    "The second time, Stella told herself Mina was probably busy."
    "The third time, Stella stood near the gate for several minutes with her bag over one shoulder, watching other students leave in little groups."
    "Mina walked past with two girls from their class."
    "She saw Stella."
